  "title": "The Hidden Culture Clues Buyers Miss (And Why They Matter)",
  "date": "2025-04-18T14:31:10.623Z",
  "content": {
    "text": "96\n\n The Hidden Culture Clues Buyers Miss (And Why They Matter)\nHi Sara,\nYou can\u2019t put company culture on a spreadsheet. But that doesn\u2019t mean you should ignore it.\nThis week, I had the good fortune of catching up with some past clients\u2014people who\u2019ve bought accounting practices and made it through their first tax season.\u00a0\nAnd when I asked, \u201cWhat do you wish you\u2019d known before closing?\u201d the biggest theme wasn\u2019t about financials, legal hurdles, or even client retention.\nIt was employee culture.\nThe Culture Trap Buyers Walk Into\nWhen you buy a business, you\u2019re often not allowed to meet employees until the deal is done. So how do you know if you\u2019re walking into a functional, well-run team\u2026 or an office that runs on chaos and quiet quitting?\nYou can\u2019t just ask, \u201cIs this a toxic workplace?\u201d (I mean, you could, but good luck getting an honest answer.) Instead, you have to get creative.\nWhat Can You Look For?\n\u279d How does the seller talk about their team?\nOne of the best signals I\u2019ve seen? A seller who voluntarily adds details about their employees beyond just salary and hours worked.\u00a0\nI once saw a seller include a column in their diligence spreadsheet that listed each employee\u2019s strengths\u2014totally unprompted. That\u2019s a seller who values their team, and that\u2019s a great sign for culture.\n\u279d How does the seller behave in negotiations?\nIf they\u2019re defensive, aggressive, or flat-out dishonest during the deal process, guess what?\u00a0\nThat\u2019s likely how they\u2019ve run the company, too. Employees don\u2019t thrive in that kind of environment.\n\u279d What\u2019s turnover like?\n Longevity can be a sign of stability\u2014but it can also mean people feel stuck and unmotivated.\u00a0\nAsk about how employees feel about leadership and why people have left in the past.\nIf Culture Isn\u2019t What You Expected\u2026\nNot every transition is smooth. Some buyers step into a workplace that isn\u2019t what they\u2019d hoped for. The good news? Culture can be fixed.\nThere are incredible CPA-specific coaches who help firms navigate post-close team issues.\u00a0(Brian Amann and Douglas Slaybaugh come to mind\u2014fantastic resources.) \nBut it takes patience and intentionality to rebuild trust and create the culture you want.\nSo, if you\u2019re in diligence right now, take an extra beat to ask the right questions. It could save you a lot of headaches after closing.\nAnd if you\u2019ve been through this already, I\u2019d love to hear from you\u2014what do you wish you had known before closing?\nTo smarter deals,\nSara Sharp\nPartner, SK&S Law Group\nBusiness Consultant & Founder of Deal Academy\nP.S. If you're looking to buy or sell an accounting firm, and want me to keep an eye out for you, fill out this form here.\n\n For more helpful lessons from Deal Academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down, where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out some recent Dealmakers podcast episodes by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2025\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
